diagnosis_text:
There is no network fault. The reported "divorce" between the 20.x (OSPF) side and the 120.x (EIGRP) side does not exist in the live lab.

r11 is the OSPF-to-EIGRP boundary router (the user's "translator box"). It runs OSPF on eth1 (111.x, Area 3, Full adjacency with r1) and EIGRP AS 100 on eth0 (112.x toward r12) and eth2 (103.x toward r13). Its OSPF process contains both `redistribute connected` and `redistribute eigrp`, which causes it to generate AS-External Type-5 LSAs for all EIGRP-learned prefixes — including 120.0.0.0/8 — and flood them into the OSPF domain. r1 (the ABR spanning Area 0 and Area 3) correctly installs 120.0.0.0/8 as an OSPF external route (distance 110, metric 20, via 111.1.1.2/r11), confirmed both from `ip route show` and from `show ip route 120.0.0.0/8` in vtysh. In the reverse direction, r12 carries a static default route via 112.1.1.1 (r11), giving the EIGRP side full reachability back into the OSPF domain without requiring explicit OSPF redistribution into EIGRP. End-to-end data-plane verification confirms bidirectional connectivity with 0% packet loss: pc0 (20.1.1.10) reaches pc12 (120.1.1.10) and vice-versa, and pc12 also reaches 10.x, 30.x, and 111.x addresses in the OSPF domain. Whatever maintenance was performed at the routing boundary last night left the network in a fully operational state. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===
